          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em rough idea
            is (but see the
            %a{:href => "#caveat"} caveat
            below) that the term
            %strong moduli space
            is essentially a synonym for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representing object
            and for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %em moduli space
            when in a context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %p
            More precisely, when a moduli space actually does exist as an ordinary space (or
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "." do
              %strong fine moduli space
          %p
            Those classifying “spaces” that are called moduli spaces are typically
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            = succeed "s." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
          %p
            So the term
            %em fine moduli space
            mainly indicates that a given object that might be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            is actually just a plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %strong coarse moduli space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            still as a plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
          %p
            A coarse moduli space is one that at least has the right underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of points as the
            %em right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            has: as long as we don’t look at families but just at single things, it does give the right information. From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            when they exist.
          %h3#caveat Caveat
          %p
            But one has to be
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong careful with this rough idea
            %strong implicit fine print
            in the notion of moduli space:
          %p
            while
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            is the term typically used for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representing object
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %em moduli space
            only if it lives in the “original” category. For more on this see the chapter
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#because"} … because they have automorphisms
          %p below.
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The term possibly originates with Riemann, who was the first to study what are now called moduli spaces of (compact)
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            %em parameter
            that parameterizes isomorphism classes of Riemann surfaces.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p …
          %h3#coarse_moduli_space Coarse moduli space
          %p
            A
            %strong coarse moduli space
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Sch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
