                            %mi h
                            %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ F &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\Psi_{M'}}{\to}&amp;&amp; h_{M'} \\ &amp; {}_{\Psi_M}\searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow \\ &amp;&amp; h_M}
          %p
            So a coarse moduli space is one that at least has the right underlying set of points as the
            %em right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            has: as long as we don’t look at families but just at single things, it does give the right information. From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            when they exist.
          %h2#because “… because they have automorphisms.”
          %p A widespread slogan is
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> Slogan
              \: A type of objects that has nontrivial
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
          %p
            The typical example motivating and illustrating this slogan is the example of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            with a non-trivial automorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : E \to E
            one may build, for instance, the family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \to S^1 \times S^1
            of elliptic curves over the torus which is obtained by starting with the trivial family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \times S^1 \times [0,1] \to S^1 \times [0,1]
            over the cylinder and then by gluing the two ends by identifying the fiber using the automorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %em locally trivial
            but globally nontrivial.
          %p
            Such locally trivial but globally non-trivial families can in principle not be classified by a representing object in the category of spaces that we are working with, as long as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            which we use to say what a locally trivial family is is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+coverage"} subcanonical
          %p
            for assume that a classifying object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            exists in our category and assume that at least one globally nontrivial but locally trivial family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \to X
            exists in the category.
          %p
            Then by definition of local triviality there exists a covering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            to each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            is equivalent to the trivial family over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(U_i, K)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
            is a covering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            in a subcanonical topology, then it follows from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            property of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(-,K)
            that there is a unique element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(X,K)
            whose restriction to all the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            represents the trivial family. But the trivial family over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has this property and hence must be that unique element. Accordingly, it would follow that there is no non-trivial family classified by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            cannot exist under the given assumptions.
          %p Notice well the two assumptions that were made to make this argument work:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the topology with which we glue families must be subcanonical: this is the assumption that fails for the situations in which one would speak of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %p
                For instance
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
              %p
                But of course it is a standard fact that there there are
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} U(n)
                such that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                classes of continuous maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathcal{B} U(n)
                classify isomorphism classes of rank-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                – representable in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Top
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Rank
                      %mi n
                      %mi VectBund
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi iso
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mrow
                          %mi Ho
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Top
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Rank n VectBund(X)/iso  \simeq Hom_{Ho(Top)}(X, \mathcal{B} U(n))
                      \\,.
              %p
                This might make the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} U(n)
                look like something that deserves to be called a fine moduli space. But one should beware that the standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+coverage"} subcanonical
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                is not so in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ho
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Top
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mrow
                          %mi Ho
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Top
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{Ho(Top)}(-, \mathcal{B} U(m))
                has a non-standard opinion about what it means to glue two topological spaces to a third one. That’s how in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Top
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top)
                the above argument that “automorphisms prevent a classifying object” breaks down.
              %p
                In fact, there is a theorem by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"} Danny Stevenson
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Michael+Roberts"} David Michael Roberts
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                and Danny Stevenson that shows that large classes of
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
              %p
                But these examples also indicate why it may be misguided to think of the “classifying spaces” such as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} U(n)
                as being moduli spaces in the first place: the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Top
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top)
                in fact regards its objects – even though they are presented as
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} U(n)
                are not classifying spaces, but are classifying
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %li
              %p
                Another situation that makes the above argument break down is if the category of “spaces” that one works with is such that gluing trivial families by nontrivial automorphisms never produces globally nontrivial families. For instance because the category of “spaces” is such that no nontrivial families exist at all. This happens for instance in the case that we take a “space” to be simply a (finite, say)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                of (finite) sets every fiber bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \to X
                (i.e. every surjection such that all fibers are isomorphic to a typical fiber
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                ) are necessarily globally trivial in that they are isomorphic to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \times X \to X
                %em is
                a “fine moduli space” in this context (inside the category of non-necessarily finite sets), namely the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                of natural numbers: the family
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E} \to X) \in Set
                with typical fiber the
                = succeed "-element" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
